WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Hi everyone, it's Jerry.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.760
I have a beautiful and instructive 17-move game to share with you.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:17.020
This was played in 2003 at the Under-14 World Youth Championship.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:26.520
On the white end, 13-year-old Jon Ludvig Hammer, rated 2074, playing against 12-year-old

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:32.840
International Master Magnus Carlsen, rated 2450.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.560
Let's have a look.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.400
We have a hyper-modern start to this one.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.960
Black says go ahead, occupy the center.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.280
I'll look to chip away at it later.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:54.000
King-bishops contribute, kings are tucked away, more pressure on white's center.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.360
The reply here, c3.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.960
In general, black welcomes d5.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:10.520
Not because this knight can advance and counterpunch, that just happens to be an added bonus.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:15.160
Bigger picture idea is that this bishop is happy.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:20.520
Its scope has been extended with the pawn now on d5.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.440
This is a point black will next look to challenge.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.080
In this game, it's the simple, solid c3.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.760
e5, coordination on d4.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.420
It's adequately defended.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:43.640
White ensures that this knight will be around to secure the center points.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.280
No worries now of a pin.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.760
Move 8, the start of an offensive.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.200
Knight h5, the halfway point of the game.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.240
Now in preparing for this video, I made several passes through this game.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:07.000
My very first pass, I saw the ending of the game and I thought, that's beautiful.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.120
And then as I played through it a few more times, I started to notice a few different

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:17.000
patterns within just these handful moves.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.440
I wonder if you can maybe pick up on what these patterns are.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.400
So first of all, what is happening with this last move?

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.840
How might we describe it?

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:33.000
Black is coordinating with his furthest advanced pawn.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.160
f4 is one square, and the other is d4.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.000
d4 is red hot.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.800
Hit three times, defended twice.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.280
White needs to do something about d4.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.380
White decides on the capture, d takes e5.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:58.000
There are three ways black can recapture in this position.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.120
What would be your move in this position?

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.400
Feel free to pause the video.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.160
Okay, Carlsen's move, knight f4.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:14.160
No recapture, no routine recapture here.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.560
Carlsen is attentive to a vulnerable point in white's camp.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.280
The bishop is under fire.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:27.840
Also h3, coordinated pressure there.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.560
White goes to b5.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.720
The knight now takes on e5, which makes this bishop a bit silly now.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:40.680
It's sort of a staring off into space bishop.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:46.720
Also there's coordination on d3, a possible jump there.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.260
Computer suggests knight c4 as a follow-up.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:54.000
In this game it is knight takes knight.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.680
There are now two ways black can recapture.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.480
What would be your move in this position?

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.320
Feel free to pause the video.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:07.160
Okay, did I get you again?

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:13.520
The very best move, no routine recapture again.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:19.340
Queen g5 takes advantage of the weakness of white's last move.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:21.520
What is going on here?

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:27.320
Carlsen is coordinating with his furthest advanced piece, the king knight.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.160
There's a mate threat on g2.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.300
The g2 pawn is pinned.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.100
Queen f3 to defend on g2 is not working.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.420
Knight takes h3 is there.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:42.620
Anything more happening?

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:49.140
Yes, the queen x-rays this staring off into space bishop.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:51.740
White plays in the best way.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.900
Knight g4.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.300
Queen takes bishop.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:00.500
Black is still coordinating with this knight.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:05.540
We have e2 and d3.

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:09.660
The suggestion by the engine in this position, the move played in the game first of all is

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.920
knight b3.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:16.760
The suggestion by the engine is to flick in a3.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:20.820
The queen on b5 is a very flexible piece.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:29.180
She does, after all, patrol a fifth rank, a completely opened fifth rank.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:38.620
If a4 is played, it suggests giving a check and then sneaking into d3.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:42.140
Slight advantage here still for team black.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:43.940
No a4 though.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:50.140
In this one, knight b3, knight e2, check.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:51.140
King h1.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:55.820
The knight has cut the queen off from defending the knight.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:58.820
Bishop takes knight now.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:04.860
A recaptures, and Carlsen just goes after a weak link.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:05.860
Pawn on e4.

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:09.980
Not easy to defend this guy.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:16.500
Yet again, the computer suggests a4 here in reply to that.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.500
Queen goes to c4.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:20.100
That's what's being recommended.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:23.160
a4 is not played in this game.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.180
Bishop e3 is on board.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:33.380
Before we look at bishop e3 though, I want to point out some variations.

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:38.340
Suppose white defends e4 directly.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:41.100
So for instance, f3.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.420
Why is that not working?

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:46.260
Well, there'd be knight g3 now with the fork.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:54.460
Well, what about the queen going to c2 or maybe even d5 to defend e4?

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:57.860
Let's put on one of the moves.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:06:59.620
There's knight g3.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.900
The queen no longer defends the knight.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:03.580
There's a trick.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:06.100
Black will win the exchange.

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:11.700
Finally, suppose the knight retreats to defend this pawn.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.340
How can black play here?

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:20.540
It says you could go ahead and simply take the bishop.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:28.460
Queen recaptures to defend the pawn, and it likes playing on the king side with h5.

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:34.780
There's even lines here where the king could go to g7, rook to h8, and looking for mate

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:35.860
on the h-file.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:39.140
There's a lot of pressure here.

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:41.900
Threat to take the knight, take on e4.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:44.980
If you play here, there's bishop to e3.

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:48.980
Black is in a winning position.

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:55.900
So no knight d2, it's just the simple bishop development.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:59.060
Rook takes, e4 follows.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:06.260
If in this position, white creates a flight square with g3, there's knight takes g3 with

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:11.620
check, pawn takes, and rook takes bishop, completely winning for black.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:16.220
Up two pawns, everything is better here for black.

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:20.180
In this game, some pressure on the knight.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:22.620
Final pop quiz.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:26.780
Can you spot Carlsen's 17th move?

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:28.700
Feel free to pause the video.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:32.420
Okay, final move.

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:36.860
Queen h5, white resigns.

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:40.220
Queen sacrifice for the win.

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:50.860
If pawn captures queen, we would have rook h4, mate, Anastasia's mate.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:52.860
What did you think of this one?

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.700
There were a few parts of this game that struck me.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:56.700
1.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:00.660
Note how there were multiple cases of no routine recaptures.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:05.420
Instead, a more forceful move was played.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:06.580
2.

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:14.820
Note the attention that was given to unprotected pieces, Carlsen targeting white's king bishop

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:16.780
on multiple occasions.

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:18.060
3.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:26.260
Note how Carlsen found ways to coordinate with his furthest advanced pawn, e5, and piece

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:33.820
king knight when it was on f4, and even in this final position, e2.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:42.420
And of course, the aesthetic ending, a queen sacrifice leading to Anastasia's mate.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.840
Who doesn't enjoy that?

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:46.540
Maybe Hammer?

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:53.020
Anyhow, feel free, as usual, to leave any feedback to this video in the comments section

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:54.020
below.

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:58.220
I hope you enjoyed it, and maybe took a thing or two away.

00:09:58.220 --> 00:09:59.580
That's all for now.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:00.580
Take care.

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:01.080
Bye.

